  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead dissolved in one inning, and they never got it back.",
    "what_this_means": "Tatis Jr. kept the Padres afloat with two home runs, and Ty France ignited a four-run eighth that pulled them within one. But the Cubs held on 9\u20137, and the gap in the division widened to twelve games. The offense generated thirteen hits and still could not finish. That is the story now. Sears lasted 4.2 innings and left early. A familiar shape for the rotation. While Marinaccio and Peralta combined for 3.2 innings of uneven work. The Padres were never ahead after the second. They chased from there. A 2\u20130 lead after one inning, a 6\u20132 deficit by the end of the second, and despite the bats returning in the third and eighth, the math never worked. They had the moment. They didn't have the finish.",
    "what_to_watch": "This team is learning that scoring in runners doesn't matter if the other team scores more. Tatis Jr.'s two home runs represent exactly what should have been enough. The lineup was active, runners moved, runs crossed. But the eighth-inning rally, the four runs that made it a game again, arrived too late to change anything. When France drew the walk that started the surge, the Cubs were already holding a 9\u20135 lead. The comeback was real. The result was still a loss. Sears' short outing set the tone. The pitching couldn't afford an anemic offense, and the offense, when it arrived, couldn't afford pitching that wobbled early. That tension is tightening. They've lost four straight. The formula works until both sides fail in the same game.",
